// Mission 03: the KQL I ran in Sentinel → Logs
// Times in UTC. Most of this mission happens off the workstation (scanning, SMB
// enumeration from my attack machine, GPP decryption) and does NOT show up here —
// only the RDP reconnection, the lockout-era failed logons, and the anonymous SMB
// access to WKS-L58 itself are workstation-visible.

// Is my machine still sending? (check before trusting "nothing found")
Heartbeat
| where Computer startswith "WKS-L58"
| summarize LastSeen = max(TimeGenerated)

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Step 2: anonymous/guest SMB access to WKS-L58
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

// Any anonymous or guest network logon (type 3) on WKS-L58
SecurityEvent
| where Computer has "WKS-L58"
| where EventID == 4624
| where LogonType == 3
| where Account has_any ("ANONYMOUS LOGON", "Guest")
| project TimeGenerated, Computer, Account, IpAddress, LogonType, AuthenticationPackageName
| order by TimeGenerated asc

// Result: 3 matches —
//   27/10 11:36:47  WKS-L58\Guest              10.50.0.1  NTLM  (first smbclient -L -N pass)
//   27/10 15:34:17  WORKGROUP\ANONYMOUS LOGON  10.50.0.1  NTLM  (true null-session retry)
//   27/10 15:35:15  WKS-L58\Guest              10.50.0.1  NTLM  (second guest retry)
// IpAddress is 10.50.0.1 for all three — the Tailscale subnet router's gateway
// address, not my actual attack-machine address. The router masks the real
// source; a real investigation would need the router's own connection logs to
// attribute this further.

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Step 4: lockout-era failed logons and the successful reconnection
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

// Failed logons for svc_deploy while troubleshooting the domain-vs-local address form
SecurityEvent
| where Computer has "WKS-L58"
| where EventID == 4625
| project TimeGenerated, Computer, Account, TargetUserName, LogonType, IpAddress, FailureReason, SubStatus
| order by TimeGenerated asc

// Result: 22 matches, every single one Account = HAMILTON\svc_deploy
//   27/10 16:43:19 - 17:03:00  (14 attempts, first troubleshooting session)
//   28/10 09:31:46, 09:31:49   (2 attempts)
//   28/10 10:10:38, 10:10:43   (2 attempts, continuing)
//   ... (22 total)
// Every event: FailureReason %%2313, SubStatus 0xC0000064 = STATUS_NO_SUCH_USER.
// Not a bad-password code — confirms WKS-L58's local SAM has no domain-qualified
// "svc_deploy" account to find at all, matching the GPP "Local Users and Groups"
// finding from Step 3. Mix of LogonType 3 (IP "-") and LogonType 10 (IP 10.50.0.1)
// both failed identically, ruling out anything RDP-protocol-specific.

// The successful RDP reconnection once addressed as a local account
SecurityEvent
| where Computer has "WKS-L58"
| where EventID == 4624
| where LogonType == 10
| where Account has "svc_deploy"
| project TimeGenerated, Computer, Account, IpAddress, LogonType, AuthenticationPackageName
| order by TimeGenerated asc

// Result: 4 matches, all Account = WKS-L58\svc_deploy (the local form, as expected)
//   28/10 09:32:35.269  (logged twice — Windows commonly logs a pair of 4624s per
//   28/10 10:11:21.472   RDP connection)
// IpAddress 10.50.0.1 again (gateway masking, same as Step 2).
// AuthenticationPackageName: Negotiate.
// This is the direct Sentinel-side confirmation of the local-vs-domain-account
// fix: HAMILTON\svc_deploy never worked because it doesn't exist; WKS-L58\svc_deploy
// worked on the first real attempt.

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Step 5: whatever the workstation logged during the session on the regained desktop
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

// Process creation during the RDP session window, if auditing covers it
SecurityEvent
| where Computer has "WKS-L58"
| where EventID == 4688
| where isnotempty(CommandLine)
| where TimeGenerated > datetime(2026-10-28 09:00)
| project TimeGenerated, SubjectUserName, NewProcessName, ParentProcessName, CommandLine
| order by TimeGenerated asc

// Not pulled in detail for the write-up — the Step 4 query above already
// establishes the RDP session bounds (09:32 / 10:11 on 28/10), and process-level
// auditing wasn't the focus once the three desktop artifacts (proof flag, new
// Sentinel credential, staged employees.csv) were already accounted for directly
// from the desktop itself.

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Addendum: further SMB/LDAP checks against WKS-L58 and the DC, off-box
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// None of this is KQL - it's attack-machine SMB/LDAP activity, noted here only
// so this file stays a complete map of "what was checked and how":
//   - WKS-L58's own Public share (not just 10.50.0.50's) also anonymously
//     exposes an account-access.txt with a second training01 credential.
//   - svc_deploy's decrypted credential is denied on WKS-L58's ADMIN$/C$ over
//     SMB (NT_STATUS_ACCESS_DENIED) - expected Windows "Remote UAC" token
//     filtering for a non-RID-500 local admin account on a *network* logon;
//     the same account has a full admin token over the *interactive* RDP
//     logon used in Step 4/5, which is why that worked and this doesn't.
//   - training01 against the DC (10.50.0.10): NETLOGON share is genuinely
//     empty live (matches the local SYSVOL download), C$/ADMIN$ both denied
//     (expected for a low-privilege account), and LDAP/389 is unreachable
//     ("Can't contact LDAP server") - consistent with Step 3's finding that
//     the DC's AD-specific ports are firewalled off this network path.
